.TH APACTION 3
.SH NAME
sigregister, sigunregister \- assign a anticipation point handler to an instruction address
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <ap.h>
.PP
.BI "int APregister(uintptr_t " APaddr ", const struct APaction *" action ");"
.PP
.BI "int APunregister(uintptr_t " APaddr ", const struct APaction *" action ");"
.IR file ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
The system defines a set of Anticipation Points (APs) that may be bound to specific RISC-V instruction addresses and triggered at runtime.
AP delivery resembles the occurrence of a hardware interrupt: the current process context is saved, any APs specified in the handler's
.I mask
are blocked from triggering, and the handler is invoked.
A process may specify a handler to which an AP is delivered, or specify that an AP is to be ignored.
Multiple handlers may be bound to the same instruction address; each handler is appended to an ordered list and invoked in that order when the AP triggers.
AP handlers normally execute with other APs blocked according to the mask specified in the
.B APaction
structure.
The mask for a given handler is initialized at registration time via
.BR APregister ().
.PP
The
.BR APregister ()
function assigns a handler for the instruction at address
.IR APaddr .
If
.I action
is non-null, it specifies a handler routine and mask to be associated with that address.
Multiple calls to
.BR APregister ()
with the same
.I APaddr
append each new handler to the execution list for that address; no previously registered handler is overwritten.
The caller must retain the pointer to
.I action
if it intends to unregister the handler at a later point.
.PP
The
.BR APunregister ()
function removes the handler specified by
.I action
from the execution list associated with
.IR APaddr .
The
.I action
pointer must be the same pointer passed to the corresponding
.BR APregister ()
call.
If no matching handler is found for the given address, the call fails and returns \-1.
.PP
The
.B APaction
structure is used by
.BR APregister ()
to specify the handler and associated options for a given instruction address.
It contains the following members:
.TP
.B APhandler_t handler
Specifies the action to take when the AP triggers.
The value may be
.B AP_IGN
to ignore the AP, or a pointer to a user-defined handler function.
.TP
.B APset_t mask
Specifies a set of APs to block for the duration of the handler's execution.
Note that APs are not automatically blocked during handler execution unless explicitly included in
.IR mask .
.TP
.B int flags
A bitmask of flags controlling handler behavior.
The following flags are defined:
.RS
.TP
.B AP_RESTART
If this bit is set, any interrupted operation
.B corrupt
modifies files by toggling a randomly chosen bit.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-bits =\fIBITS\fR
Set the number of bits to modify.
Default is one bit.


.SH RETURN VALUES
The
.BR APregister ()
and
.BR APunregister ()
functions return the value 0 if successful; otherwise the value \-1 is returned and the global variable
.I errno
is set to indicate the error.
